    {
      "title": "Booker T Washington",
      "text": "Booker T Washington was born into slavery in Franklin County, Virginia, in 1856. At the end of the Civil War, he went to work in salt and coal mines, attending school when he could.\n\nHe worked throughout his early life to become educated. As an adult, he became an educator himself and turned the Tuskegee Institute into an acclaimed school for vocational and industrial education. The school focused on teaching African Americans to set aside political demands and focus on making economic gains.\n\nWashington’s position earned him the trust and support of many white businessmen and politicians, including President Theodore Roosevelt, but it angered many of his fellow African American citizens.",
      "image": "https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcR8MexMP-dCJEq37OjnxFV7iAQNCOg3LGGaT_nMeUeV2feacq5o7dpjx1M:https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51R02NKC4OL._AC_UF894,1000_QL80_.jpg&s=10",
      "set": "DuBois and Washington",
      "_order": 7
    },
    {
      "title": "WEB DuBois",
      "text": "Among those who opposed Washington’s compromising approach was WEB DuBois. Unlike Washington, DuBois was born in the North after the end of the Civil War and received an excellent education, graduating high school at the age of 16. He won a scholarship to Fisk University in Tennessee, where he first witnessed the poverty, violence, and discrimination that many African Americans experienced in the South.\n\nWhat DuBois experienced led him to commit his life to education and activism. Critical of Washington’s focus on vocational education and disregard for political rights, DuBois began writing and speaking out against racist practices. He urged African Americans to organize and demand full recognition of their rights as citizens.\n\nIn 1905, he formed the Niagara Movement to work for civil rights, and four years later, he helped to start the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP). Over the course of his life, DuBois wrote 20 books and hundreds of articles on history, politics, and other matters.",
      "image": "https://aaregistry.org/wp-content/uploads/2009/09/webdubois.jpg",
      "set": "DuBois and Washington",
      "_order": 8
    },
